(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cardiomyopathy-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Cardiomyopathy Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 20-year-old man with acute myocarditis after a flulike illness is on the ward with heart failure symptoms. He reports new palpitations, and his nurse notes a brief episode of lightheadedness. Which complication poses the most immediate threat to his life?","choices":["Aortic dissection","Pericardial thickening","Mitral valve prolapse","Ventricular arrhythmia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In this young man with myocarditis after a flulike illness, new palpitations and a brief episode of lightheadedness point to the arrhythmic danger: palpitations, syncope or sudden cardiac death in myocarditis are due to underlying ventricular arrhythmias or atrioventricular block. Sudden death makes this the most immediate threat. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with chest pain and breathlessness after a recent viral illness is being evaluated for myocarditis. Her electrocardiogram shows sinus tachycardia. Which laboratory result would indicate cardiac myonecrosis?","choices":["Elevated serum troponin","A low white cell count","Normal sedimentation rate","A low natriuretic peptide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In this young woman with chest pain and breathlessness after a viral illness, serum troponin is the indicator of cardiac myonecrosis. Her sinus tachycardia fits the electrocardiogram of myocarditis, and the rest of the work-up looks for leukocytosis and a possibly elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman admitted with stress cardiomyopathy after a house fire has a repeat echocardiogram that shows a thrombus in the akinetic left ventricular apex. She is in sinus rhythm. Which treatment is now indicated?","choices":["Nitrate infusion","Surgical myectomy","Anticoagulation","Pacemaker insertion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of Takotsubo cardiomyopathy is empiric and supportive and depends on the presentation, and a thrombus in her akinetic left ventricular apex is the finding that calls for anticoagulation. Beta blockers are helpful, and diuretics are added only if heart failure is present; most patients completely recover. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with idiopathic dilated cardiomyopathy has taken a beta blocker, an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor, spironolactone and a diuretic for many months. She still has breathlessness on moderate exertion, and a repeat echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 25%. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Implantable cardioverter defibrillator","Endomyocardial biopsy to find a cause","Surgical myectomy of the septum","Stopping the beta blocker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. She has had many months of maximum medical therapy (beta blocker, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor, spironolactone and a diuretic), and her ejection fraction is still 25%. An implantable cardioverter-defibrillator is indicated when the ejection fraction is below 35% after maximum medical therapy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with restrictive cardiomyopathy and marked biatrial enlargement has had palpitations for a week, and the electrocardiogram now shows atrial fibrillation. Which treatment reduces her risk of embolism?","choices":["Surgical myectomy","A positive inotrope","Anticoagulation","Aggressive diuresis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In this woman with restrictive cardiomyopathy, marked biatrial enlargement and new atrial fibrillation, anticoagulants may be needed to prevent embolism from atrial thrombi. Beta blockers or verapamil and diltiazem may help filling time, but only anticoagulation addresses the embolic risk. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man with suspected viral myocarditis has an elevated troponin and normal coronary arteries. His cardiologist wants to see how much of the heart muscle is inflamed without an invasive procedure. Which test shows the extent of inflammation?","choices":["Gadolinium-enhanced cardiac magnetic resonance imaging","Ambulatory rhythm monitoring for forty-eight hours","Exercise echocardiography with treadmill testing","Right heart catheterization with pressure tracings"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For this man with suspected viral myocarditis, a raised troponin and normal coronary arteries, gadolinium-enhanced magnetic resonance imaging is the noninvasive test that shows the inflammation: gadolinium improves its visibility and shows its extent. Endomyocardial biopsy, the gold standard, is invasive. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman was admitted with chest pain after a near drowning. Her electrocardiogram showed ST elevation with T-wave inversion, and echocardiography showed hypokinesis of the midsegment and apex of the left ventricle. Which catheterization finding is expected if this is stress cardiomyopathy?","choices":["A proximal coronary occlusion","A ruptured coronary plaque","Triple-vessel narrowing","Normal coronary arteries"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A 70-year-old woman with chest pain after a near drowning, ST elevation with T wave inversion, and hypokinesis of the midsegment and apex of the left ventricle fits stress cardiomyopathy. The diagnosis is confirmed with cardiac catheterization, which most often shows normal coronary arteries. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is admitted after surgery with low blood pressure, and a resident proposes a drug to strengthen her heart's contraction. Which drug class should be avoided in her?","choices":["Beta blockers such as metoprolol","Antiarrhythmics such as amiodarone","Calcium channel blockers like verapamil","Positive inotropes such as digoxin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In her obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, a drug to strengthen contraction is exactly what to avoid: positive inotropes such as digoxin, epinephrine and norepinephrine increase contractility, which increases the risk of myocardial ischemia and may induce arrhythmias. Beta blockers do the reverse, decreasing contractility. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman with a week of fever and myalgia now has severe breathlessness. She is cold and clammy with a blood pressure of 78/50, heart rate 128, raised jugular venous pressure and crackles throughout both lungs. Myocarditis is suspected. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Exercise testing to grade severity","Aggressive hemodynamic stabilization","Outpatient follow-up in one week","Elective biopsy before any support"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. She is hypotensive at 78/50, tachycardic, cold and clammy with raised jugular venous pressure and crackles, a patient in shock. Supportive care is the mainstay of myocarditis treatment, directed toward the clinical scenario, and this scenario requires aggressive hemodynamic stabilization first. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and outflow tract obstruction has breathlessness and chest tightness on exertion. His blood pressure is 124/78 and heart rate is 76. He takes no cardiac medication yet. Which is the initial medication for his outflow obstruction?","choices":["A high-intensity loop diuretic","A beta blocker such as metoprolol","A nitrate such as nitroglycerin","A positive inotrope such as digoxin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For his outflow tract obstruction with exertional breathlessness and chest tightness, a beta blocker such as metoprolol is the initial medication. As a negative chronotrope and inotrope it reduces heart rate and enhances diastolic filling by reducing ventricular stiffness and decreasing contractility; verapamil or diltiazem is the alternative. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man with restrictive cardiomyopathy from cardiac sarcoidosis has two episodes of syncope. His heart rate is 34, and the electrocardiogram shows complete heart block. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Surgical myectomy","Alcohol septal ablation","Endomyocardial biopsy","Permanent pacemaker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His syncope, heart rate of 34 and complete heart block fit conduction system disease: in restrictive cardiomyopathy, fibrosis of the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes can lead to heart block. A permanent pacemaker is the treatment for this conduction disease. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has just been diagnosed with symptomatic idiopathic dilated cardiomyopathy and an ejection fraction of 25%. He asks what his long-term outlook is. Which statement about his prognosis is most accurate?","choices":["Nearly all recover fully in weeks","Worst outlook of any heart muscle disease","A normal life span without therapy","About half die within five years"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. For a man with newly symptomatic idiopathic dilated cardiomyopathy, prognosis depends on response to medical therapy but is poor overall, with 50% mortality at 5 years once symptoms emerge. Deaths come largely from the progressive nature of the disease and sudden cardiac death due to arrhythmia. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 45, 99, 104"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with stable cardiomyopathy on full medical therapy says he tires after walking a block and feels anxious about exercising. He has no symptoms at rest. Which referral is most appropriate to improve his exercise tolerance?","choices":["A competitive sports clearance clinic","A cardiac rehabilitation program","Hospice referral for end-stage care","Genetic screening of his children"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His problems are limited exercise tolerance and anxiety about exercising, and cardiac rehabilitation programs aim to improve exercise tolerance and quality of life. Rehabilitative care also provides education on medication adherence, nutrition and symptom monitoring, and psychological support for coping with chronic illness. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with restrictive cardiomyopathy has fatigue and exercise intolerance along with leg edema, hepatomegaly and ascites, but her lungs are relatively clear. Which pattern of heart failure is expected to dominate in this condition?","choices":["Isolated left-sided failure","High-output heart failure","Pure systolic failure","Right-sided heart failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her leg edema, hepatomegaly and ascites with relatively clear lungs are the right-sided pattern. In restrictive cardiomyopathy, filling pressures are elevated in both ventricles, but right heart failure may dominate over left, with an elevated jugular venous pressure, bilateral pedal edema and ascites. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A 17-year-old boy with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy asks his clinician what the most serious risk of his disease is. He has occasional palpitations and plays recreational basketball. Which complication is the leading cause of death in this condition?","choices":["Embolic stroke","Aortic dissection","Sudden cardiac death","Progressive heart failure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For this teenager with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and palpitations, the most serious risk is sudden cardiac death, the leading cause of mortality in the disease. Patients are classically advised to keep to mild to moderate, noncompetitive activity, stay hydrated and avoid excess alcohol. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with suspected restrictive cardiomyopathy has right-sided heart failure and big atria on echocardiogram. Her cardiologist wants to measure pressures inside both ventricles to characterize the filling problem. Which invasive test evaluates ventricular pressure tracings?","choices":["Exercise echocardiography","Cardiac catheterization","Endomyocardial biopsy","Transesophageal echocardiogram"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. To measure pressures inside both ventricles, the cardiologist needs cardiac catheterization, which is useful in restrictive cardiomyopathy to evaluate ventricular pressure tracings. Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging adds information when the cause is unclear, and biopsy is reserved for specific situations. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with dilated cardiomyopathy has orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat), crackles at both lung bases and diminished breath sounds at the right base. A chest radiograph is ordered. Which finding on the film would indicate pulmonary edema?","choices":["Short horizontal lines meeting the pleura","A small heart with clear lung fields","A normal film that excludes congestion","Isolated thickening of the pericardium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His orthopnea and basal crackles suggest pulmonary edema, and on the chest radiograph Kerley B lines, horizontal lines 1 to 2 cm long meeting the pleura at right angles, indicate it. The diminished breath sounds at his right base fit the pleural effusion and cardiomegaly that dilated cardiomyopathy also shows. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man presents with new-onset heart failure a week after a flulike illness. He smokes and has hyperlipidemia, so the team wants to exclude coronary disease before labeling this myocarditis. Which test rules out coronary ischemia as the cause of his new heart failure?","choices":["Sedimentation rate","Chest radiograph","Cardiac angiography","Endomyocardial biopsy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac angiography is used in the myocarditis work-up to rule out coronary ischemia as a cause of new-onset heart failure. That matters for this man, whose smoking and hyperlipidemia give him coronary risk, before his heart failure after a flulike illness is labeled myocarditis. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has restrictive cardiomyopathy confirmed on imaging, but cardiac magnetic resonance imaging and genetic testing have not identified a cause. His team is considering endomyocardial biopsy. When is biopsy performed in this condition?","choices":["Only after cardiac transplantation is done","Never, since it cannot change management","When imaging and genetic tests are negative","As the first test in every suspected case"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In restrictive cardiomyopathy, endomyocardial biopsy is performed when all imaging studies and genetic testing are negative, or when a tissue sample is likely to confirm or exclude a diagnosis that would change management. His negative magnetic resonance imaging and genetic testing meet the first condition. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has new dilated cardiomyopathy with an ejection fraction of 30%. He reports fatigue, heat intolerance and weight loss, and the conjunctivae look pale. Which initial laboratory tests are most useful for finding a contributing cause?","choices":["Blood cultures and a throat swab","Urine osmolality and cortisol","Thyroid function and a blood count","Serum lipase and amylase levels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. His heat intolerance and weight loss raise hyperthyroidism, which is on the dilated cardiomyopathy differential, and his pale conjunctivae raise anemia. The initial work-up includes thyroid function and a complete blood count to evaluate for anemia, along with a metabolic panel and liver function. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is leaving for a summer hiking trip in a hot climate. She takes a beta blocker and asks how to lower her risk of symptoms while away. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Take extra diuretic on hot days","Use alcohol to relax at the campsite","Stay well hydrated; avoid dehydration","Restrict fluids to soften the murmur"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy are advised to maintain hydration and avoid dehydration, which matters on a hot-weather hiking trip. The same education covers avoiding excess alcohol and keeping to mild to moderate, noncompetitive recreational activity. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 73-year-old woman has just been diagnosed with restrictive cardiomyopathy from amyloidosis. She has right-sided heart failure and asks how her condition compares with other kinds of heart muscle disease. Which statement about her prognosis is most accurate?","choices":["Poorest of all heart muscle diseases","Complete recovery within weeks","A normal life span with diuretics","Better than other cardiomyopathies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Restrictive cardiomyopathy has the poorest prognosis among all types of heart muscle disease. Management treats the underlying disorder, such as her amyloidosis, uses diuretics with caution and anticoagulants or a pacemaker as needed, and ends with cardiac transplantation. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has right-sided heart failure with a raised jugular venous pressure and ascites. Echocardiogram shows abnormal diastolic filling, normal-sized ventricles and big atria, but the team cannot tell restrictive cardiomyopathy from constrictive pericarditis. Which test is used to differentiate them?","choices":["Ambulatory rhythm monitoring","Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging","Repeat transthoracic echocardiogram","Exercise stress echocardiogram"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Echocardiography alone is not definitive because restrictive cardiomyopathy and constrictive pericarditis look alike, as in this man with big atria and abnormal diastolic filling. Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging is used to differentiate them, showing pericardial thickening in pericarditis. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 15-year-old boy is seen for a sports physical. He has no symptoms, but his father was recently diagnosed with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, which is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern. Which strategy for this adolescent is most appropriate?","choices":["Genetic screening for the condition","Waiting until adulthood to evaluate","A routine chest radiograph each year","An endomyocardial biopsy at this visit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Age-specific care for adolescents includes genetic screening for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, which fits a 15-year-old whose father has the autosomal dominant disease. Activity restriction is added for high-risk individuals, with medication for symptoms and an implantable defibrillator in select cases. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has heart failure symptoms that have gradually progressed over several years despite treatment. She asks what option exists if her disease stops responding to conventional therapy. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["Genetic testing to guide therapy","Cardiac transplantation","Digoxin to boost contraction","Daily nitrates for comfort"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In hypertrophic cardiomyopathy heart failure symptoms gradually progress, as hers have over several years, and cardiac transplantation is the option when the disease is unresponsive to conventional therapy. Sudden cardiac death remains the leading cause of mortality. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 75, 77, 82"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy still has disabling breathlessness despite a beta blocker and later verapamil. Echocardiogram shows systolic anterior motion (the anterior mitral leaflet pulled into the outflow tract) with mitral regurgitation. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker alone","Long-term nitrate therapy","Escalating loop diuretics","Surgical septal myectomy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her obstruction is refractory to a beta blocker and verapamil, so surgical myectomy, which excises part of the septal myocardium causing outflow obstruction, is the next step. Replacement of the mitral valve may or may not be needed, because the mitral regurgitation may resolve with myectomy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has new palpitations. Her pulse is irregularly irregular and the electrocardiogram shows atrial fibrillation. Echocardiogram shows left atrial enlargement. Which treatment prevents embolic stroke in her?","choices":["Anticoagulation","Digoxin for rate control","Nitrate therapy","Diuretic therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, atrial fibrillation, as in this woman with an irregularly irregular pulse and left atrial enlargement, is treated with anticoagulation to prevent embolic stroke. Antiarrhythmics such as amiodarone convert and suppress it, and radiofrequency ablation is another option. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man is diagnosed with dilated cardiomyopathy. His father had a cardiomyopathy and his older brother died suddenly at 35. No alcohol, drug or chemotherapy exposure is found. Which is the most appropriate referral for him and his family?","choices":["Family screening only after age 60","Genetic counseling for the family","Reassurance that it is not inherited","Heart biopsies for his relatives"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His father's cardiomyopathy and his brother's sudden death at 35 are a family history, with no alcohol, drug or chemotherapy cause found. Patient education in dilated cardiomyopathy includes genetic counseling for patients with a family history, alongside removing any offending source and sodium restriction. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man with cardiomyopathy has become lightheaded on standing since his medications were increased at his last visit. He is frail and has lost weight. Which parts of his drug therapy call for particular caution at his age?","choices":["Diuretics and vasodilators","Sodium restriction advice","Home-based rehabilitation","Implanted device checks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Age-specific care for elderly patients calls for cautious use of diuretics and vasodilators, with therapy tailored to comorbidities and frailty and a focus on symptom control and quality of life. His lightheadedness on standing after a dose increase, with frailty and weight loss, is the situation that caution targets. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man who has drunk heavily for 20 years has six months of worsening breathlessness on exertion and orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat). The apical impulse is displaced laterally and a third heart sound (an early diastolic sound just after the second heart sound) is heard. Echocardiogram shows dilated, thin-walled ventricles with an ejection fraction of 25%, and coronary angiography shows no significant narrowing. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Stress cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dilated, thin-walled ventricles with an ejection fraction of 25% and no significant coronary narrowing fit dilated cardiomyopathy: myocardial dysfunction with dilation and impaired contraction in the absence of severe occlusive coronary artery disease. The result is systolic dysfunction, here in a man with 20 years of heavy drinking. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 17"}]};
